Z||000000||DaRkLoRD||09-29-2000||11:24 AM||||while trying to stay awake in one of my boring classes this morning (electro-mechanical controls.. we were calculating resistance values.. *yawn* boring class.) I started thinking about the exhaust system I saw on a Corvette.. I wondered if I could do a true dual exhaust setup with the Fiero, by mounting the mufflers underneath the trunk area, in the middle. (I realize I'd lose a few inches of trunk space.. that's ok.) depending on the measurements, I'd probably do a crossover tube setup, to minimize tubing complexity.. (does that make sense? I got 2 hours sleep last night and I'm not thinking too clearly at the moment)<P>Anyway, my question is.. how is exhaust flow calculated? the goal is better mid range power, but also increased torque. Would ported manifolds and a dual exhaust allow this? I know porting the manifolds decreases torque slightly, but how would a dual exhaust system affect this change? do mufflers state the flow rate they have? or does this need to be calculated?<P>thanks!<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||5
Z||000001||ThatRickGuy||09-29-2000||03:43 PM||||Dual exhaust may reduce torque, it matters on the size of the pipes. I'm not sure on the formula to calculate the proper width, but I know the trick for how to find the right place for the x pipe  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> cheep paint and a week of driving. spray paint the exhaust, and drive it for a while, the paint will buble/peel/crack or just generally look really bad until a specific point. just behind that is where you want the junction to be. I would guess arround a 1-1/2 to 2 inch pipe would be good. with that kind of set up, yes, you're going to lose torque, but, you're horsepower will raise (as long as you don't go too extreme). interesting idea tho.||||reg||1
